<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Sabines</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 290 BCE (approx.) · capital Cures</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Italic people of the hills north-east of Rome, central to Roman foundation legends, who were absorbed by Rome in the early 3rd century BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Sabines lived in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and the hill country between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aniene">Aniene</a> from the early 1st millennium BCE. Ancient writers saw them as the parent stock of several Sabellian peoples, such as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/samnite-league">Samnites</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/picentes">Picentes</a>, who were said to have left Sabine land in 'sacred springs' of migration.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sabine society was rural, made up of villages and small towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cures">Cures</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rieti">Reate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norcia">Nursia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiternum">Amiternum</a>, with a reputation in Roman eyes for austere virtue. They spoke an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osco-umbrian-languages">Osco-Umbrian</a> language known only from glosses. In Roman legend the abduction of the Sabine women led to joint rule between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> and the Sabine king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-tatius">Titus Tatius</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome's</a> second king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numa-pompilius">Numa Pompilius</a> came from Cures. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudia-gens">Claudii</a> were said to descend from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attus-clausus">Attus Clausus</a>, who moved to Rome around 504 BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manius-curius-dentatus">Manius Curius Dentatus</a> conquered the Sabines in 290 BCE. They received citizenship without the vote, and full citizenship in 268 BCE, an early and complete absorption into the Roman state. Their land was later part of Augustus's Regio IV, and the name survives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabina">Sabina</a> district north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sabines.jpg" alt="Sabines" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur (talk) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Latium_-5th_Century_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 290 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cures</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sabine polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sabine</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sabini</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-545&amp;focus=sabines" aria-label="Sabines on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4988 677 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sabines lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5308 933L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5308 933h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5308 933h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Cures. <a href="/atlas?year=-545&amp;focus=sabines">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

